the pursuit for the truth behind the  deadliest fire in irish history  survivors and relatives of the victims  of the stardust disaster gathered in  dublin as part of their campaign for  answers  on the evening of valentine's day 1981  48 young people were killed and more  than 200 injured when a fire ripped  through the stardust nightclub in artane  patdon lost her 21 year old brother  brian  such was the intensity of the blaze her  family couldn't bury his remains just  some clothing she remembers the family  receiving the news my mom was a great  woman lovely lady very stoked and she  kind of just sat there  my dad just lost the head  screamed and cried no no no can't be him  it was their baby they almost have seven  so though  the rest of that was called kind of a  blur and we sat down and dad just kept  crying mum just sat there in silence  white face and the rest of us just  couldn't comprehend what had happened  so what caused the star dust fire well  an inquiry in the immediate aftermath  found that the probable cause was arson  that hugely angered the survivors which  saw it as a form of victim blaming it  wasn't until 2009 that a new report  found there was no evidence for arson  then in 2019 the irish government  ordered a new inquest to be held into  the 48 deaths but that still hasn't  started  legal arguments and an issue surrounding  the juror pay has delayed the new  inquest but for samantha mangan whose  mother helena was killed it can't wait  any longer i have five beautiful  children and i can't be the best mum to  them until i know what's happening  because this is like a brick and it's  killing me it feels like there's a  chain around my neck you know what i  mean and i just can't move forward until  i find out what happened to her and why  she didn't come home  the new inquest will be the largest and  longest of its kind in irish history  possibly lasting for four years it  should get underway sometime in march  and bring the stardust families a step  closer to the end of their decades-long  search for answers  stephen murphy sky news in dublin
 

